{{smaller block|Peaches ''tinged'' with the odorous bitter of their pits, and clear as amber. {{right|''R. T. Cooke'', Somebody's Neighbors, p. 40.}}}}

3. To modify by intermixture or infusion; vary the tone or bent of.

{{smaller block|Our city-mansion is the fairest home,<br>But country sweets are ''ting'd'' with lesser trouble.{{right|''Quarles'', Emblems, iv. 7.}}}}

{{smaller block|Words . . . serene,<br>Yet ''tinged'' with infinite desire<br>For all that might have been.{{right|''M. Arnold'', Obermann Once More.}}}}

'''tinge''' (tinj), ''n.'' [< ''tinge'', ''v.''] 1. A slight or moderate degree of coloration; a shade or tint of color; a modification of hue, tint, or complexion.

{{smaller block|Autumn bold,<br>With universal ''tinge'' of sober gold.{{right|''Keats'', Endymion, i.}}}}

{{smaller block|Her skin was fair, with a faint ''tinge'', such as the white rosebud shows before it opens.{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Professor, iii.}}}}

2. A modifying infusion or intermixture; a shade of some qualifying property or characteristic; a touch, taste, or flavor.

{{smaller block|The stories [of the common people of Spain] . . . have generally something of an Oriental ''tinge''.{{right|''Irving'', Alhambra, p. 188.}}}}

'''tingent''' (tin′jent), ''a.'' [< L. ''tingen(t-)s'', ppr. of ''tingere'', dye, tinge: see ''tinge''.] Having power to tinge; tinting. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|As for the white part, it appears much less enriched with the ''tingent'' property. {{right|''Boyle.''}}}}
